B.Yp=new A.aK("pdf_12_1","12. Code of Signals A\u2013Z","International Code of Signals \u2014 Single-Letter Signals (A to Z)","Standard single-letter meanings in the International Code of Signals (ICS):",null,B.S3)
B.YM=new A.aK("pdf_13_1","13. Quick Interview Advice","Quick Interview Advice for Maritime Technical & Orals","\u2022 For any technical subject, start with the definition, then principle/working, then one practical example.\n\u2022 For safety questions, structure the answer as: raise alarm \u2192 inform responsible officer/Master \u2192 assess situation \u2192 take immediate safe action \u2192 use correct equipment/procedure \u2192 report/record.\n\u2022 For navigation questions, know the units: nautical mile, knot, degrees/minutes, latitude/longitude, course and bearing.\n\u2022 For machinery questions, know basic purpose, working principle, major parts, common faults and safety precautions.\n\u2022 If you do not know an answer in an interview, do not invent it. Say what you know and explain how you would verify the correct procedure.",null,null)
B.Yt=new A.aK("pdf_14_1","14. Personal HR Questions","Introduce yourself and explain why you want to join the Merchant Navy.","Present your full name, academic background (PCM Class 12th / Pre-sea training), family background, interest in maritime discipline, passion for navigation or marine engineering, and mental & physical readiness for a disciplined life at sea.","Keep your self-introduction structured: 1. Academic qualifications, 2. Key achievements, 3. Motivation for merchant navy, 4. Immediate career target.",null)
B.Zu=new A.aK("pdf_14_2","14. Personal HR Questions","Why did you choose to apply for our specific shipping company?","Highlight the company's global fleet reputation, safety management system (SMS), global trade routes, structured cadet sponsorship & promotion path, and commitment to environmental sustainability.",null,null)
B.YT=new A.aK("pdf_14_3","14. Personal HR Questions","What are your key strengths and one weakness you are working to improve?",'Strengths: Discipline, adaptability under pressure, strong teamwork, and technical curiosity. Weakness: Mention a real skill you are actively improving (e.g. "I am practicing advanced celestial navigation calculations every day to improve speed").',null,null)
B.Yf=new A.aK("pdf_14_4","14. Personal HR Questions","Where do you see yourself in 5 to 7 years in your maritime career?","Outline a disciplined career path: Completing pre-sea cadetship, obtaining Class 4 / OOW Certificate of Competency, serving as 3rd Officer / 4th Engineer, and preparing for Chief Officer / 2nd Engineer CoC examinations.",null,null)
B.ZA=new A.aK("pdf_14_5","14. Personal HR Questions","How do you prepare mentally for spending long contracts (6-9 months) away from family?","Explain mental resilience, maintaining daily shipboard routines, staying focused on professional learning and safety, taking part in onboard sports/recreation, and staying in regular contact with family during port calls.",null,null)
B.YC=new A.aK("pdf_15_1","15. Psychometric Questions","What would you do if a senior officer ignores a safety rule during deck or engine room operations?","Respectfully bring the potential safety risk to their attention using Stop Work Authority / safety protocol principles. If safety is immediately compromised, intervene safely and report through official SMS safety reporting channels.","Safety is non-negotiable at sea. Interviewers look for courage combined with professional respect.",null)
B.Zd=new A.aK("pdf_15_2","15. Psychometric Questions","How do you handle high-stress situations with tight deadlines during port operations?","Prioritize safety first, communicate clearly with watchkeepers, break down tasks into structured steps using checklists, remain calm under pressure, and never compromise safety procedures for speed.",null,null)
B.ZE=new A.aK("pdf_15_3","15. Psychometric Questions","Describe how you resolve interpersonal conflicts with fellow crew members on board.","Listen actively to understand their perspective, keep emotions neutral, focus on common vessel operational goals, and follow proper chain of command if conflict affects ship safety or morale.",null,null)
B.Zr=new A.aK("pdf_15_4","15. Psychometric Questions","How do you adapt to working in a multicultural crew from diverse nationalities?","Embrace cultural diversity, use standard maritime English for clear unambiguous communication, respect different traditions and dietary habits, and maintain positive team spirit.",null,null)
B.Zc=new A.aK("pdf_15_5","15. Psychometric Questions","What action should you take if you experience extreme fatigue while on navigational or engine watch?","Inform the Officer of the Watch (OOW) or Chief Engineer immediately. Working while fatigued impairs alertness and violates STCW rest hour regulations and vessel SMS safety policies.",null,null)
B.Z4=new A.aK("pdf_16_1","16. Seagull / CES Questions","Seagull CES: What is the SOLAS requirement for fire and abandon ship drills on cargo vessels?","Every crew member shall participate in at least one abandon ship drill and one fire drill every month. Drills must take place within 24 hours of leaving port if more than 25% of the crew have not participated in drills on that ship in the previous month.","Seagull CES tests exact regulatory thresholds. Memorize SOLAS Chapter III drill intervals.",null)
B.Z2=new A.aK("pdf_16_2","16. Seagull / CES Questions","Seagull CES: On what frequencies does a modern EPIRB transmit distress signals?","An EPIRB transmits a 406 MHz digital distress signal to the Cospas-Sarsat satellite system and a 121.5 MHz homing signal for search and rescue craft, accompanied by a high-intensity white strobe light.",null,null)
B.Zo=new A.aK("pdf_16_3","16. Seagull / CES Questions","Seagull CES: MARPOL Annex V rules for comminuted food waste discharge outside Special Areas.","Food waste ground or comminuted to pass through a screen with openings no greater than 25 mm may be discharged into the sea while the vessel is en route and not less than 3 Nautical Miles from the nearest land.",null,null)
B.YF=new A.aK("pdf_16_4","16. Seagull / CES Questions","Seagull CES: What is the operating depth of a hydrostatic release unit (HRU) for liferafts?","A Hydrostatic Release Unit (HRU) automatically cuts the painter/lashing line to release the liferaft container at a submerged depth of between 1.5 and 4.0 meters.",null,null)
B.Ze=new A.aK("pdf_16_5","16. Seagull / CES Questions","Seagull CES: What triggers the Safety Contour alarm on an ECDIS?","The Safety Contour separates safe navigable water from shallow water based on ship draft, squat, and required Under Keel Clearance (UKC). Approaching or crossing the safety contour triggers a visual and audible alarm.",null,null)
B.Yo=new A.aK("zodiac_pdf_1","Zodiac Maritime Real Interview Questions","Zodiac Interview: Why did you choose Merchant Navy over government jobs?","Highlight your passion for maritime navigation/engineering, disciplined work culture, exposure to cutting-edge vessel technology, hands-on operational leadership, and clear merit-based CoC career progression.","Show confidence and clarity. Emphasize why sea life suits your long-term career ambition.",null)
B.YS=new A.aK("zodiac_pdf_2","Zodiac Maritime Real Interview Questions","Zodiac Interview: What specific PPE must be worn while climbing a ship ladder or mast?","Full PPE required: Safety harness with double lanyards (hooked to a secure point above waist level), safety helmet with chin strap, non-slip safety boots, work gloves, and boiler suit.",null,null)
B.Z9=new A.aK("zodiac_pdf_3","Zodiac Maritime Real Interview Questions","Zodiac Interview: Difference between an Oil Purifier and a Clarifier.","Purifier: Separates two liquids of different densities (water and fuel oil) plus heavy solids using a gravity disc. Clarifier: Separates fine solid impurities from liquid (no water separation, uses a blind disc).",null,null)
B.YA=new A.aK("zodiac_pdf_4","Zodiac Maritime Real Interview Questions","Zodiac Interview: How does a ship Refrigeration System work and what are its 4 main components?","The 4 main components are: 1. Compressor (compresses refrigerant gas), 2. Condenser (cools and liquefies refrigerant), 3. Expansion Valve (meters flow and drops pressure), 4. Evaporator (absorbs heat from cold rooms).",null,null)
B.YZ=new A.aK("zodiac_pdf_5","Zodiac Maritime Real Interview Questions","Zodiac Interview: What happens when you close the discharge valve of a Centrifugal Pump vs a Positive Displacement Pump?","Centrifugal Pump: Liquid churns inside casing and pressure rises to shutoff head; pump can run closed briefly. Positive Displacement Pump: Pressure rises continuously until relief valve opens or discharge line/casing ruptures. Discharge valve must NEVER be closed on startup.",null,null)
B.Zl=new A.aK("zodiac_pdf_6","Zodiac Maritime Real Interview Questions","Zodiac Interview: Explain the working of an Oily Water Separator (OWS) and 15 PPM monitor.","OWS uses gravity separation and coalescer filter elements to separate oil from bilge water. The 15 PPM bilge alarm monitor continuously checks oil content; if >15 PPM, a 3-way solenoid valve automatically diverts discharge back to the bilge holding tank.",null,null)
B.Z0=new A.aK("zodiac_pdf_7","Zodiac Maritime Real Interview Questions","Zodiac Interview: Explain the Fire Tetrahedron and its 4th component.","The Fire Tetrahedron consists of 4 components: Fuel, Heat, Oxygen, and the 4th component: Uninhibited Chemical Chain Reaction. Halon, Dry Chemical Powder, and specialized agents extinguish fire by breaking this chain reaction.",null,null)
B.YU=new A.aK("zodiac_pdf_8","Zodiac Maritime Real Interview Questions","Zodiac Interview: Types of fixed fire fighting systems used onboard ships.","1. High-Pressure Hyper-Mist System (local protection for machinery spaces), 2. Bulk Total Flooding CO\u2082 System (for engine rooms and cargo holds), 3. High-Expansion Foam System (for boiler rooms and bilges).",null,null)
B.Zt=new A.aK("zodiac_pdf_9","Zodiac Maritime Real Interview Questions","Zodiac Interview: How does a Turbocharger work?","A Turbocharger utilizes exhaust gas energy from engine cylinders to drive a turbine wheel. The turbine drives a centrifugal compressor wheel on the same shaft, compressing ambient air and delivering high-density scavenge air to engine cylinders.",null,null)
B.Zk=new A.aK("zodiac_pdf_10","Zodiac Maritime Real Interview Questions","Zodiac Interview: Working principle of a Vacuum Flash Fresh Water Generator (FWG).","Operates under high vacuum (approx 93% / 700 mmHg) created by an air ejector. The vacuum lowers seawater boiling point to around 45\xb0C, using main engine jacket cooling water (65-80\xb0C) as the heating medium to produce pure distillate fresh water.",null,null)
B.YB=new A.aK("zodiac_pdf_11","Zodiac Maritime Real Interview Questions","Zodiac Interview: What is a Rocker Arm and its function?","A Rocker Arm is an oscillating lever that receives force from the camshaft pushrod/hydraulic medium and transmits it to depress and open the cylinder intake or exhaust valve against valve spring pressure.",null,null)
B.Zy=new A.aK("zodiac_pdf_12","Zodiac Maritime Real Interview Questions","Zodiac Interview: Key differences between 4-Stroke and 2-Stroke engines.","4-Stroke Engine: Completes 1 power cycle in 4 piston strokes (720\xb0 crankshaft rotation); uses inlet and exhaust valves. 2-Stroke Engine: Completes 1 power cycle in 2 piston strokes (360\xb0 crankshaft rotation); uses scavenge ports and exhaust valve/ports.",null,null)
B.Zi=new A.aK("zodiac_pdf_13","Zodiac Maritime Real Interview Questions","Zodiac Interview: Man Overboard (MOB) Marker specifications and operation.","MOB marker is attached to bridge wing lifebuoys. When released, it automatically emits dense orange smoke for at least 15 minutes and a 2-candela white light for at least 2 hours to mark the location of a casualty.",null,null)
B.Ym=new A.aK("nyk_pdf_1","NYK Line Real Interview Questions","NYK Interview: What is the difference between an automobile engine and a marine engine?","\u2022 Environment & Medium: Marine engines operate in harsh saltwater environments requiring corrosion-resistant materials (bronze, cupronickel) and closed-loop fresh water cooling cooled by seawater heat exchangers, whereas car engines use air-cooled radiators with antifreeze.\n\u2022 Load Profile: Marine engines run continuously at heavy constant load (80\u201390% MCR) for days/weeks, whereas automobile engines experience frequent speed and load variations.\n\u2022 Size & Power: Marine engines range from medium-speed 4-stroke generator engines to massive low-speed 2-stroke propulsion engines operating on HFO/VLSFO with direct shaft coupling, whereas automobile engines are high-speed petrol/diesel engines with complex gearboxes.\n\u2022 Safety & Redundancy: Marine engines feature duplicate critical systems (duplex filters, standby pumps, oil mist detectors) with strict SOLAS/IMO compliance.",null,null)
B.Yu=new A.aK("nyk_pdf_2","NYK Line Real Interview Questions","NYK Interview: Classify the types of internal combustion (IC) engines.","\u2022 By Working Cycle: 2-Stroke engines (1 power stroke per crank rev / 360\xb0) and 4-Stroke engines (1 power stroke per 2 crank revs / 720\xb0).\n\u2022 By Fuel & Ignition: Spark Ignition (SI - Petrol/Gas) and Compression Ignition (CI - Diesel/HFO).\n\u2022 By Cylinder Layout: In-line, V-engine, Radial, Opposed Piston.\n\u2022 By Engine Speed: High-speed (>1000 RPM), Medium-speed (300\u20131000 RPM), Low-speed (<300 RPM).\n\u2022 By Construction: Trunk Piston engines vs Crosshead engines.\n\u2022 By Induction: Naturally Aspirated vs Turbocharged / Supercharged.",null,null)
B.Yx=new A.aK("nyk_pdf_3","NYK Line Real Interview Questions","NYK Interview: What is the difference between a spark plug and a fuel injector? Where and why is each used?","\u2022 Spark Plug:\n  - Function: Creates a high-voltage electrical spark across an electrode gap to ignite an air-fuel mixture.\n  - Where used: Petrol (gasoline) & Gas engines (Spark Ignition / SI).\n  - Why used: Petrol has higher self-ignition temperature and lower volatility under compression, requiring an external electrical arc to initiate combustion at TDC.\n\u2022 Fuel Injector:\n  - Function: Atomizes liquid fuel into fine droplets under high hydraulic pressure (200\u20132000 bar) and delivers it directly into compressed hot air.\n  - Where used: Diesel and Heavy Fuel engines (Compression Ignition / CI).\n  - Why used: Air is compressed to high temperature (~500\u2013700\xb0C) exceeding self-ignition temperature of diesel, causing auto-ignition upon fuel injection.",null,null)
B.Yl=new A.aK("nyk_pdf_4","NYK Line Real Interview Questions","NYK Interview: Classify electric motors.","\u2022 AC Motors (Alternating Current):\n  - Induction / Asynchronous Motors: Squirrel Cage Induction Motor (most widely used onboard for pumps, fans, compressors due to ruggedness and low maintenance) and Slip Ring / Wound Rotor Motor.\n  - Synchronous Motors: Run at exact synchronous speed (Ns = 120f/P), used for high-power electric propulsion and shaft generators.\n\u2022 DC Motors (Direct Current): Series Motor (high starting torque for winches/cranes), Shunt Motor (constant speed), Compound Motor.\n\u2022 Specialized Motors: Brushless DC (BLDC) motors, Stepper motors, and Servo motors for control actuators and governor positioning.",null,null)
B.ZC=new A.aK("nyk_pdf_5","NYK Line Real Interview Questions","NYK Interview: At what point in the cycle is fuel injected into a diesel engine? Explain the 4-stroke valve timing diagram.","\u2022 Fuel Injection Timing: Starts slightly BEFORE Top Dead Center (TDC) during compression stroke (typically 10\xb0 to 25\xb0 BTDC) to allow for ignition delay, ensuring peak combustion pressure is reached shortly after TDC (approx. 5\xb0 to 10\xb0 ATDC).\n\u2022 4-Stroke Valve Timing Diagram Summary:\n  - Suction Valve: Opens ~10\xb0\u201320\xb0 BTDC, Closes ~30\xb0\u201340\xb0 ABDC (uses gas momentum for max volumetric efficiency).\n  - Fuel Injection: Begins ~10\xb0\u201320\xb0 BTDC, Ends ~10\xb0\u201315\xb0 ATDC.\n  - Exhaust Valve: Opens ~35\xb0\u201345\xb0 BBDC (blowdown of exhaust gases), Closes ~15\xb0\u201320\xb0 ATDC.\n  - Valve Overlap: Period around TDC where both inlet and exhaust valves are open simultaneously to scavenge the cylinder and cool internal parts.",null,null)
B.Yq=new A.aK("nyk_pdf_6","NYK Line Real Interview Questions","NYK Interview: What is apparent slip?","\u2022 Definition: Apparent slip is the percentage difference between theoretical propeller speed (Pitch x RPM) and actual vessel speed relative to seabed (Va).\n\u2022 Formula: Apparent Slip (%) = [(Theoretical Distance - Observed Ship Distance) / Theoretical Distance] x 100.\n\u2022 Significance: Can be positive or negative depending on ocean currents, wind, sea state, and hull fouling. Real slip accounts for wake fraction of water moving with vessel.",null,null)
B.Yh=new A.aK("nyk_pdf_7","NYK Line Real Interview Questions","NYK Interview: Explain working principle of a fuel oil purifier. Can HFO and MDO be separated using a purifier?","\u2022 Working Principle: Centrifugal separator runs at high rotational speed (6000\u20138000 RPM) creating high G-force. Liquid entering disc stack separates by density differences: Water (heavy phase) forced outwards, Clean Oil (light phase) displaced inwards, and Solid Sludge thrown to bowl periphery.\n\u2022 Can HFO & MDO be separated if mixed?: NO. HFO and MDO are miscible liquid hydrocarbons (they form a homogenous liquid solution). A purifier only separates immiscible liquids of different densities (water from oil) and insoluble solid particles.",null,null)
B.ZF=new A.aK("nyk_pdf_8","NYK Line Real Interview Questions","NYK Interview: Are auxiliary engines used for shipboard generators the same as standard 4-stroke diesel engines?","\u2022 Yes, structurally they are 4-stroke trunk piston CI engines operating on Otto/Diesel thermodynamic cycle.\n\u2022 Marine Adaptations: Fitted with electronic/hydraulic governors maintaining constant speed (720 RPM for 60 Hz or 1000/1500 RPM for 50 Hz), HFO preheating/recirculation systems, jacket water preheating, SOLAS double-walled high-pressure fuel lines with leak alarm, and marine safety shutdowns (overspeed, low lube oil pressure, high cooling temperature).",null,null)
B.Z5=new A.aK("nyk_pdf_9","NYK Line Real Interview Questions","NYK Interview: Are 4-stroke engines used on modern commercial ships? If yes, where and why?","\u2022 YES, extensively used onboard modern ships:\n  1. Auxiliary Diesel Generators: On bulkers, tankers, container ships, and gas carriers to produce shipboard electricity.\n  2. Main Propulsion: On cruise ships, ferries, Ro-Ro vessels, tugs, and OSVs in multi-engine diesel-electric or geared setups.\n\u2022 Why Used: Compact footprint and low headroom requirements compared to tall 2-stroke engines, high power-to-weight ratio, flexible speed control, and ability to run multiple smaller generator sets to match fluctuating electrical loads efficiently.",null,null)
B.Zw=new A.aK("nyk_pdf_10","NYK Line Real Interview Questions","NYK Interview: Fire Emergency Scenario \u2014 Immediate steps, extinguisher selection, no extinguisher nearby, definition & F.I.R.E. protocol.","\u2022 A. Immediate Steps for Generator Fire: 1. Raise Emergency Alarm / Shout Fire in Engine Room. 2. Inform Bridge & Chief Engineer. 3. Stop affected Generator (Emergency Stop) & trip fuel Quick-Closing Valve (QCV). 4. Isolate electrical breaker. 5. Evacuate non-essential crew & attempt initial attack if safe.\n\u2022 B. Extinguisher Selection: CO\u2082 (Carbon Dioxide) or Dry Chemical Powder (DCP). CO\u2082 is preferred for electrical/generator fires as it leaves no residue and does not damage electrical windings.\n\u2022 C. Action if No Extinguisher Nearby: Retrace steps safely keeping escape route open, sound manual alarm call point, trip fuel QCVs, close ventilation dampers/doors to starve fire of oxygen, and prepare main fire hose line or activate Local Application Hyper-Mist / Fixed CO\u2082 flooding.\n\u2022 D. Definition & F.I.R.E. Protocol: Fire is a rapid exothermic oxidation chemical reaction producing heat, light, and reaction products. F.I.R.E. protocol stands for: F = Find fire, I = Inform / Raise alarm, R = Restrict / Contain fire (close doors/vents/valves), E = Extinguish / Evacuate.",null,null)
B.Yk=new A.aK("nyk_pdf_11","NYK Line Real Interview Questions","NYK Interview: State Archimedes' Principle.","\u2022 Statement: Any body completely or partially submerged in a fluid experiences an upward buoyant force equal to the weight of the fluid displaced by the body.\n\u2022 Formula: Fb = \u03c1 x V x g (where \u03c1 is fluid density, V is submerged volume, g is acceleration due to gravity).\n\u2022 Maritime Application: Explains ship buoyancy and flotation; vessel displaces a volume of seawater whose total weight equals total displacement mass of vessel.",null,null)
B.Yd=new A.aK("nyk_pdf_12","NYK Line Real Interview Questions","NYK Interview: What is the speed of sound in air versus water? Why does sound travel faster in water than in air?","\u2022 Speed of Sound: In Air ~343 m/s (at 20\xb0C). In Seawater ~1480\u20131500 m/s (approx. 4.5 times faster than in air).\n\u2022 Why Faster in Water: Sound is a mechanical longitudinal wave propagating through particle collisions. Water is a liquid with much higher density and bulk modulus (incompressibility) than air. Molecules in water are packed much closer together, transferring acoustic vibration energy rapidly compared to widely separated, highly compressible air molecules.",null,null)
